# NX563J Bluetooth bring-up (WCN3990 "Cherokee", HCI over BLSP1_UART3 = /dev/ttyHS0)
#
# Ground truth (2026-09-09, live on device):
#   - stock Nubia 4.4.194 kernel also has CONFIG_BT_HCIUART unset: its BT
#     stack (bluedroid) talks HCI through the wcnss_filter USERSPACE daemon
#     reading /dev/ttyHS0 directly. For a Linux/BlueZ userspace we want the
#     standard kernel hci0 instead -> enable the hci_uart line discipline
#     with the QCA (IBS) protocol.
#   - TLV rampatch/NVM download is done in userspace by the stock vendor
#     binary hci_qcomm_init (crbtfw21.tlv + crnv21.bin from the bluetooth
#     partition, sde22). The 4.4-era in-kernel hci_qca.c (IBS only, no TLV)
#     is exactly right for this split.
#   - The BT block inside the WCN3990 package is only released at chip POR
#     when the BT rails (btpower rfkill0) are already on. Therefore
#     wifi-bringup4.sh unblocks rfkill0 BEFORE the modem boots the chip.
#   - Power/clock/pinctrl/UART verified stock-identical (stock DTB from
#     backups/2026-09-07-baseline/boot.img, board v2.1); UART DM loopback
#     passes; hci_qcomm_init completes TLV download and reads the chip MAC
#     (00:a0:c6:c3:c9:3a) once the above POR condition is met.

CONFIG_BT_HCIUART=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART_H4=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART_QCA=y

# Useful profiles for a mini-server (stock leaves these off; we want them)
CONFIG_BT_RFCOMM=y
CONFIG_BT_RFCOMM_TTY=y
CONFIG_BT_BNEP=y
CONFIG_BT_BNEP_MC_FILTER=y
CONFIG_BT_BNEP_PROTO_FILTER=y
CONFIG_BT_HIDP=y
